---
import TreatmentPage from "../../../components/TreatmentPage.astro";

const T = "/services/fertility-treatment";
const A = "/services/assessment";

const page = {
  slug: "ovulation-test",
  name: "Ovulation Assessment",
  title: "Ovulation Assessment in Lahore | Dr. Kamran Saleem",
  description: "Ovulation assessment in Lahore with Dr. Kamran Saleem: cycle review, LH surge guidance, progesterone confirmation and follicle ultrasound, with a clear plan.",
  eyebrow: "Ovulation Assessment in Lahore",
  h1: "Ovulation Assessment in Lahore",
  waText: "Hello, I would like to book an ovulation assessment with Dr. Kamran Saleem.",
  ctaLabel: "Book an ovulation assessment",
  heroSub: [
    "An ovulation assessment works out whether you ovulate, how regularly, and when in your cycle it happens. Ovulation problems are among the most common and most treatable causes of delayed conception, so this is often one of the first questions to answer.",
    "With Dr. Kamran Saleem, the assessment starts with your cycle history and any kit or app results you have, then adds blood work or ultrasound only if it will give you a clear answer.",
  ].join(" "),
  leadIn: [
    { rich: ["Ovulation assessment is one part of a complete fertility evaluation. What is advised depends on your cycle pattern, symptoms and earlier results. See the full ", ["Fertility Assessment & Diagnosis", A], " pathway, or read our guide to ", ["tracking your ovulation", "/resources/ovulation-test"], "."] },
  ],
  sections: [
    {
      id: "what-ovulation-assessment-clarifies",
      title: "What an Ovulation Assessment Clarifies",
      intro: "The goal is a clear answer to three questions: are you ovulating, how regularly, and when.",
      icon: "calendar",
      body: [
        "Ovulation is the release of an egg from an ovary, usually once per cycle. The egg survives for roughly a day, while sperm can survive in the reproductive tract for up to about five days. This is why intercourse in the days leading up to ovulation gives the best chance of pregnancy.",
        "A regular cycle usually, but not always, means ovulation is happening. Irregular cycles do not always mean there is no ovulation, only that its timing is harder to predict. A clinician reviews your whole picture before deciding what, if anything, to add.",
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "signs-ovulation-may-be-affected",
      title: "Signs Ovulation May Be Irregular or Absent",
      intro: "These patterns are worth discussing, although none of them confirms a problem on its own.",
      icon: "clipboard",
      body: [
        { ul: [
          "Cycles that are shorter than about 21 days or longer than about 35 days",
          "Periods that vary widely in timing from month to month",
          "No period for three months or more without pregnancy",
          "No clear LH surge on home ovulation kits over several cycles",
          "Signs of PCOS, such as acne, excess hair growth or weight gain, or a known thyroid or prolactin problem",
          "Trying to conceive for 12 months, or 6 months if you are 35 or older",
        ] },
        "Light or very painful periods, cycle changes after stopping contraception, and sudden weight or stress changes can also affect ovulation, and are worth mentioning at your consultation.",
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "how-ovulation-is-assessed",
      title: "How Ovulation Is Assessed: Methods Compared",
      intro: "Each method answers a different question, so the right one depends on your situation.",
      icon: "compare",
      body: [
        { ul: [
          "Cycle history and tracking: period dates, cervical mucus changes and, optionally, basal body temperature show a pattern over several months",
          "Home urine LH kits detect the hormone surge that usually comes about one to two days before ovulation, which helps with timing",
          "Mid-luteal progesterone, taken about a week before the expected period, helps confirm whether ovulation probably occurred",
          "Ultrasound follicle monitoring follows follicle growth across the cycle and can show a follicle maturing and releasing an egg",
          "Related hormone work such as TSH, prolactin, FSH, LH and AMH when the history points to a hormonal cause",
        ] },
        "A positive LH kit shows a hormone surge, not proof that an egg was released. This is why progesterone or ultrasound is added when a definite answer matters. Phone apps that only predict ovulation from period dates are the least reliable method, especially with irregular cycles.",
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "using-ovulation-kits",
      title: "Using Ovulation Predictor Kits Well",
      intro: "Kits are useful for timing when they are used correctly.",
      icon: "flask",
      body: [
        { ul: [
          "Follow your brand’s instructions, as schedules and sensitivity differ",
          "Start from your usual cycle length, not a fixed calendar date",
          "Use the kit at about the same time each day and avoid large amounts of fluid beforehand",
          "Intercourse on the day of a positive result and the day after is generally advised",
          "Record your results and bring them to your consultation",
        ] },
        "With PCOS or very irregular cycles, kits can show several surges or none at all, which makes them hard to read. If several cycles give no clear pattern, ask a clinician rather than repeating the same approach.",
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "causes-of-irregular-ovulation",
      title: "Common Reasons Ovulation Is Affected",
      intro: "Ovulation depends on several hormones working together, so there is more than one possible explanation.",
      icon: "flask",
      body: [
        { ul: [
          "Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS), the most common cause of irregular ovulation",
          "Thyroid disease or raised prolactin",
          "Large weight gain or loss, very intense exercise or prolonged stress",
          "Reduced ovarian reserve or the approach to menopause",
          "Certain medicines, or recent stopping of hormonal contraception",
        ] },
        { rich: ["Read more in ", ["PCOS and Fertility", "/resources/pcos-and-fertility"], " and ", ["Hormone Test for Fertility", "/resources/hormone-test--for-fertility"], ", or see our ", ["hormonal analysis", `${A}/hormonal-analysis`], " service."] },
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "preparing-for-your-visit",
      title: "How to Prepare for Your Ovulation Assessment",
      intro: "A little preparation makes the consultation far more useful.",
      icon: "route",
      body: [
        { ul: [
          "Write down the first day of your last six periods, or export them from your tracking app",
          "Bring any kit results, temperature charts and previous scans or reports",
          "List medicines and supplements, including any hormonal contraception you have used",
          "Note symptoms such as pain, heavy bleeding, acne or hair changes",
        ] },
        "If you are asked to attend on a specific cycle day for blood work or an ultrasound, the clinic will explain the timing when you book.",
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "after-assessment",
      title: "What Happens After the Assessment",
      intro: "You leave knowing whether you are ovulating and what to do about it.",
      icon: "route",
      body: [
        { ul: [
          "A plain explanation of your findings",
          "Advice on timing intercourse around your fertile days",
          "A decision on further investigations, if any are needed",
          "If ovulation is affected, a discussion of options in the context of your wider evaluation",
        ] },
        { rich: ["For the wider picture, see the ", ["female fertility assessment", `${A}/female-fertility-assessment`], ", and for a partner, the ", ["male fertility assessment", `${A}/male-fertility-test`], ". If treatment becomes relevant, you can explore our ", ["fertility treatment", T], " services."] },
      ],
    },
  ],
  faqs: [
    { q: "What is an ovulation assessment?", a: "It is a clinical review of whether and when you ovulate. It combines your cycle history with, where needed, ovulation kit results, progesterone measurement or follicle monitoring on ultrasound." },
    { q: "How can I tell if I am ovulating?", a: "Regular cycles, cervical mucus changes and a positive LH kit all suggest it, but none of them proves it. Mid-luteal progesterone or ultrasound monitoring gives a firmer answer." },
    { q: "Can a home ovulation kit confirm that I ovulated?", a: "No. A urine LH kit detects the hormone surge that usually comes before ovulation, but it does not show that an egg was released. Progesterone or ultrasound is used when confirmation is needed." },
    { q: "When should I use an ovulation kit?", a: "Start according to your usual cycle length and the kit instructions. If your cycles are irregular, ask a clinician how to plan testing, because the fixed schedules on kits may not suit you." },
    { q: "Can I ovulate and still have irregular periods?", a: "Yes. Some women ovulate late or at varying times, which makes periods irregular. Others ovulate regularly with a cycle length that is simply shorter or longer than average." },
    { q: "Can I ovulate without having a period?", a: "It can happen before a first period or before periods return after pregnancy or contraception. Absent periods otherwise deserve medical review." },
    { q: "Do I need an ultrasound to check ovulation?", a: "Not everyone does. Ultrasound monitoring is advised when it answers a specific question, such as very irregular cycles or repeated unclear kit results." },
    { q: "What causes irregular or absent ovulation?", a: "PCOS is the most common cause. Others include thyroid disease, raised prolactin, large weight changes, intense exercise, stress and reduced ovarian reserve." },
    { q: "Can irregular ovulation be treated?", a: "Often, yes. Treatment depends on the cause and may include lifestyle changes, treating a thyroid or prolactin problem, or medicines to stimulate ovulation. The clinician explains the options after your assessment." },
    { q: "When should I see a doctor about ovulation?", a: "If your cycles are irregular or absent, or if you have been trying to conceive for 12 months, or 6 months if you are 35 or older. Seek advice sooner if you already have a known condition such as PCOS." },
  ],
  closingHeading: "Ovulation Assessment with Dr. Kamran Saleem",
  closingParas: [
    "Dr. Kamran Saleem will review your cycle history and any results you bring, explain whether further investigation is appropriate and outline realistic next steps. The plan depends on your individual circumstances.",
    "You can ask about consultation fees and any separate investigation costs before confirming your appointment.",
  ],
  resources: [
    { href: "/resources/ovulation-test", category: "Fertility Assessment", title: "Ovulation Test: How to Track Your Cycle" },
    { href: "/resources/pcos-and-fertility", category: "Fertility Assessment", title: "PCOS and Fertility" },
    { href: "/resources/hormone-test--for-fertility", category: "Fertility Assessment", title: "Hormone Test for Fertility" },
  ],
  close: {
    heading: "Ready to Book Your Ovulation Assessment?",
    body: "Bring your cycle dates, kit results or previous reports, so your assessment can focus on your questions from the first visit.",
    cta: "Book an ovulation assessment",
  },
};
---

<TreatmentPage {...page} />
